# Nocturne edge TLS terminator.
#
# Automatic HTTPS with Let's Encrypt, fronting the internal YARP gateway.
# The only required configuration is BASE_DOMAIN (set in .env); Caddy obtains
# and renews all certificates with no API keys.
#
#   - {$BASE_DOMAIN}    the apex (single-tenant deployments live here) — cert
#                       issued via the HTTP-01 challenge.
#   - *.{$BASE_DOMAIN}  per-tenant subdomains — certs minted on demand on first
#                       request via HTTP-01. The on_demand "ask" endpoint lets
#                       the API confirm a hostname is a real tenant before Caddy
#                       requests a certificate, so only valid subdomains issue.
#   - *.share.{$BASE_DOMAIN}
#                       public share links, which are one label deeper. A Caddy
#                       site-address wildcard matches exactly one label, so this
#                       needs naming separately or share links never get a cert.
#                       The ask endpoint resolves the token before authorizing.
#
# To front Nocturne with your own reverse proxy instead, disable this service
# (see docker-compose.byo-proxy.yaml) and the gateway is exposed on plain HTTP.
#
# Caddy is the only hop that sees the real peer, so it is the only hop that may name the
# client. It overwrites X-Forwarded-For with exactly one entry, which the gateway passes
# through and the API consumes: nothing a caller writes survives, and nothing downstream
# has to guess how many entries of the chain to believe.
#
# TRUSTED_PROXIES is what makes a CDN's client header believable. Left at the default,
# only the socket peer counts, so a forged CF-Connecting-IP is ignored. Set it to the
# CDN's published ranges (Cloudflare: https://www.cloudflare.com/ips/) to have the
# visitor's address recorded instead of the CDN edge's.
#
# trusted_proxies_strict is what makes that safe for a CDN that appends to
# X-Forwarded-For rather than sending CF-Connecting-IP. Without it Caddy falls back to the
# leftmost valid entry of the chain — the one a caller prepends — and would stamp it as the
# definitive client. Strict walks from the right instead, skipping declared proxies, so the
# address is the first hop nobody vouched for.
{
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://nocturne-api:8080/api/v4/platform/tls-authorize
	}
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static {$TRUSTED_PROXIES:127.0.0.1/32}
		trusted_proxies_strict
		client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP X-Forwarded-For
	}
}

(to_gateway) {
	reverse_proxy gateway:5000 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.client_ip}
	}
}

{$BASE_DOMAIN} {
	import to_gateway
}

*.{$BASE_DOMAIN} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	import to_gateway
}

*.share.{$BASE_DOMAIN} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	import to_gateway
}
